[[de:Wabi-Sabi]] '''''Wabi-sabi''''' (in [[Kanji]]: &#20376;&#23490;) represents a comprehensive [[Japan]]ese world view or [[aesthetic]]. It is difficult to explain precisely in Western terms. According to [[Leonard Koren]], wabi-sabi is the most conspicuous and characteristic feature of what we think of as traditional Japanese beauty and it "occupies roughly the same position in the Japanese pantheon of aesthetic values as do the Greek ideals of beauty and perfection in the West." [[Andrew Juniper]] claims, "if an object or expression can bring about, within us, a sense of serene melancholy and a spiritual longing, then that object could be said to be wabi sabi."
===Western use=== During the [[1990s]] the concept was borrowed by computer software developers and employed in [[Agile programming]] and [[Wiki wiki]] to describe acceptance of the state of ongoing imperfection that is the product of these methods.
==References== * {{Book reference | Author=Koren, Leonard | Title=Wabi-Sabi for Artists, Designers, Poets & Philosophers | Publisher=Stone Bridge Press | Year=1994 | ID=ISBN 1880656124}} * {{Book reference | Author=Juniper, Andrew | Title=Wabi Sabi: The Japanese Art of Impermanence | Publisher=Tuttle Publishing | Year=2003 | ID=ISBN 0804834822}}
== External links == *[http://www.art.unt.edu/ntieva/artcurr/asian/wabisabi.html Japanese Aesthetics, Wabi-Sabi, and the Tea Ceremony]
[[Category:Japanese culture]] [[Category: software development process]]
{{stub}}

